(Bitcoin Whitepaper Explained) Tj
T* (Wielfried Zouantcha  |  August 8, 2026) Tj
T* (Canonical: https://www.zouantcha.com/blog/bitcoin-whitepaper-explained) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (This note explains Satoshi Nakamoto's 2008 paper "Bitcoin: A Peer-to-Peer Electronic Cash) Tj
T* (System" for readers who want the academic structure, not a trading pitch.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (Problem. Internet payments usually rely on trusted intermediaries. That model enables) Tj
T* (reversible disputes and raises the minimum practical transaction size. The paper asks) Tj
T* (whether electronic payments can rest on cryptographic proof instead of institutional) Tj
T* (trust.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (Electronic coins as signature chains. A coin is a chain of digital signatures. Ownership) Tj
T* (transfers by signing a hash of the previous transaction and the next owner's public key.) Tj
T* (Signatures prove a chain of custody, but they do not by themselves prevent) Tj
T* (double-spending.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (Double-spending without a mint. A central mint can reject second spends, but reintroduces) Tj
T* (a trusted party. The paper's alternative is a public history: transactions are broadcast,) Tj
T* (and participants converge on a single ordered ledger of which spend came first.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (Timestamp server and proof-of-work. Blocks hash transactions \(and the previous block\) into) Tj
T* (a chain. Proof-of-work, in the spirit of Hashcash, makes rewriting history expensive:) Tj
T* (changing a block requires redoing its work and the work of every block after it. Honest) Tj
T* (majority hash power makes the honest chain grow fastest.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (Network and incentives. Nodes collect transactions, mine blocks, and extend the longest) Tj
T* (valid chain. The first transaction in a block mints a coinbase reward to the miner; fees) Tj
T* (can later replace issuance. Incentives align attackers toward earning rewards rather than) Tj
T* (destroying the system's value.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (Light clients and privacy. Simplified Payment Verification \(SPV\) checks Merkle proofs) Tj
T* (against block headers rather than full history. Privacy is partial: keys can stay) Tj
T* (pseudonymous, but multi-input transactions and address reuse can still link activity.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (Security model. An attacker who rewrites recent history faces a race against the honest) Tj
T* (chain. Under honest majority, the probability of catching up from z blocks behind falls) Tj
T* (exponentially in z. Invalid transactions are still rejected by honest nodes even if an) Tj
T* (attacker mines blocks.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (How to read the original. The source paper is short. Read abstract and sections 1-4 for) Tj
T* (the design, then incentives, Merkle pruning, SPV, and the calculations appendix for the) Tj
T* (security argument.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (Canonical URL for this explanation:) Tj
T* (https://www.zouantcha.com/blog/bitcoin-whitepaper-explained) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (Primary source \(Nakamoto, 2008\): https://bitcoin.org/bitcoin.pdf) Tj
